          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real vector space of dimension $n$, let $B$ be a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form, and put $Q(v)=B(v,v)$. A vector $u$ is **anisotropic** when $Q(u)\\ne0$; a subspace $U$ is **totally isotropic** when $B|_{U\\times U}=0$. Define $O(V,B)=\\{A\\in\\operatorname{GL}(V):B(Av,Aw)=B(v,w)\\}$ and $SO(V,B)=\\{A\\in O(V,B):\\det A=1\\}$. For $U\\le V$, put $U^\\perp=\\{v:B(v,U)=0\\}$. Then:\n\n1. $\\dim U^\\perp=n-\\dim U$, and every totally isotropic $U$ has $\\dim U\\le n/2$.\n2. Every such $(V,B)$ has a basis of pairwise orthogonal anisotropic vectors.\n3. For $A\\in O(V,B)$, $\\operatorname{im}(A-I)=\\ker(A-I)^\\perp$.\n4. For anisotropic $u$, the map $r_u(v)=v-2B(v,u)u/Q(u)$ is an orthogonal reflection: it fixes $u^\\perp$, sends $u$ to $-u$, and has determinant $-1$.",
