          "quote": "Let $k$ be a field with $\\operatorname{char}(k)\\ne2$, let $V$ be a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space, and let $B:V\\times V\\to k$ be a symmetric bilinear form, not necessarily nondegenerate. Put $Q(v)=B(v,v)$. Write $T_k(V)$ for the tensor algebra and $j:V\\to T_k(V)$ for its degree-one inclusion ([[def-tensor-algebra-of-a-vector-space]]). For each $v\\in V$, set $r_v=j(v)j(v)-Q(v)1$ and let $J_Q$ be the intersection of all two-sided ideals of $T_k(V)$ containing every $r_v$ ([[def-left-right-and-two-sided-ideal]]). This family is nonempty because it contains $T_k(V)$, and its intersection is a two-sided ideal; thus $J_Q$ is the smallest such ideal.\n\nDefine the **universal Clifford algebra** with the plus-$Q$ convention by\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cl}^{+}(V,Q):=T_k(V)/J_Q.$$\n\nThe quotient operations are induced from $T_k(V)$; the two-sided ideal condition makes multiplication of cosets well defined. Let $q_Q:T_k(V)\\to\\mathrm{Cl}^{+}(V,Q)$ be the quotient map, put $i_Q=q_Q\\circ j$, and write $\\bar v=i_Q(v)$. The defining relation is $\\bar v^{\\,2}=Q(v)1$. In this pair, $\\mathrm{Cl}(V,Q)$ abbreviates $\\mathrm{Cl}^{+}(V,Q)$ after this sign convention has been fixed. No nondegeneracy assumption is imposed.",
